# secscan - find secrets in git before they are published

[![CI](https://github.com/TexAlphaWolf/sec-secrets-scanner/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/TexAlphaWolf/sec-secrets-scanner/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
[![CodeQL](https://github.com/TexAlphaWolf/sec-secrets-scanner/actions/workflows/codeql.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/TexAlphaWolf/sec-secrets-scanner/actions/workflows/codeql.yml)
[![Self scan](https://github.com/TexAlphaWolf/sec-secrets-scanner/actions/workflows/self-scan.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/TexAlphaWolf/sec-secrets-scanner/actions/workflows/self-scan.yml)
![Python 3.11-3.14](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.11%E2%80%933.14-blue)
![License MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-green)

A commit that contains an API key is a leak, even if the next commit deletes it: the key stays in the history for anyone who clones. `secscan` looks for keys, tokens and private keys in three places:

1. **The commit you are about to make** (`--staged`, also as a git hook): the cheapest moment to catch it.
2. **The working tree.**
3. **Every commit on every branch** (`--history`): where the "deleted" secrets still live.

Standard library only, no runtime dependencies. It calls the `git` you already have. It **never prints a secret**: reports show a public prefix (`ghp_`, `AKIA`) and asterisks, and there is deliberately no option to show more.

![secscan scanning a demo repository: the working tree, then the whole history](docs/demo.png)

*A demo repository with generated fake credentials. The second command finds a GitHub token that the latest commit had already removed.*

## Quick start

```bash
pip install git+https://github.com/TexAlphaWolf/sec-secrets-scanner

secscan scan                 # the working tree of the current repository
secscan scan --history       # every line ever added, on every branch
secscan install-hook         # refuse any commit that adds a secret
```

Exit code `0` means nothing found, `1` means secrets found, `2` means a usage or environment error. So it works unchanged in CI and in hooks.

- **pre-commit framework:** add `repo: https://github.com/TexAlphaWolf/sec-secrets-scanner`, `rev: v1.0.0`, `hooks: [{id: secscan}]` to `.pre-commit-config.yaml`.
- **Docker, no network, read-only:** `docker build -t secscan .` then `docker run --rm --network none --read-only -v "$PWD:/repo:ro" secscan scan`.
- **GitHub code scanning:** `secscan scan --history --format sarif -o results.sarif` and upload it; [`self-scan.yml`](.github/workflows/self-scan.yml) does exactly this on this repository.

## Adopting it on a repository that already has findings

```bash
secscan scan                       # some findings: old ones, test fixtures
secscan baseline                   # writes .secscan-baseline.json (fingerprints only, no secrets)
secscan scan                       # nothing new; the suppressed ones are counted
```

From then on only **new** secrets fail the build. A fingerprint is the rule, the path and a hash of the secret, so it survives the line moving but not the secret changing. Silence a single line with a trailing `# secscan:ignore`, a path with `.secscanignore` (one pattern per line) or `--exclude`.

If it finds a real secret: **revoke it first**. Rewriting history comes second, because the key may already have been copied. See [docs/security.md](docs/security.md).

## What it detects

20 rules ([the full list](docs/rules.md)): AWS keys, GitHub / GitLab / npm / PyPI tokens, Slack and Discord webhooks, Stripe, Google, OpenAI, Anthropic and SendGrid keys, Telegram bot tokens, JWTs, private key blocks, passwords inside URLs, and a generic rule for a quoted value assigned to a name such as `api_key` or `password`.

A regular expression alone buries you in noise, so each match then has to pass:

- an **entropy** check (random-looking, scaled to the length of the value),
- a **placeholder** check (`your_password_here`, `${TOKEN}`, `xxxx`, `AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE`, `os.environ[...]`),
- a **structure** check (identifiers, module paths and package names such as `quoted-string` or `libsecret-1-0` are not secrets).

## Measured

Everything below is reproduced by [`tools/evaluate.py`](tools/evaluate.py) and [`tools/bench_history.py`](tools/bench_history.py), and CI fails if the first two rows regress. [Method and caveats](docs/evaluation.md).

| Measurement | Result |
|---|---|
| Recall, 99,800 generated secrets in 20 formats and several kinds of line | **99.83%** (every rule except the generic one 100%; random 12-character generic secrets 99.4%) |
| False positives on 7,000 benign lines that look like secrets (UUIDs, hashes, base64, env lookups, placeholders, prose) | **0** |
| CPython 3.13 standard library (300k lines) | 1 finding, a documentation example |
| 14,000 files of third-party packages (3.9M lines) | 7 findings; none is a live credential: one test private key, six fake values in tests and docs |
| Speed, working tree | about 120,000-160,000 lines per second, one thread |
| Speed, history (2,000 commits, 200,000 added lines) | about 2.5 seconds |

The first runs flagged 20 findings in the standard library and 15 in the third-party code; 19 and 8 of them were false positives (identifiers such as `token_type = 'quoted-string'`, package names, module paths), which led to the structure check. That tuning used those corpora, so the numbers on them are optimistic; the [evaluation notes](docs/evaluation.md) say which checks were independent.

## Designed for hostile repositories

Scanning someone else's repository is a normal use, so the scanner treats the repository as untrusted input:

- It **never executes anything from the repository**: git is called with fixed arguments and no shell, with `textconv`, external diff drivers and `fsmonitor` disabled (a repository can configure those to run a program; a test proves they do not run).
- It **does not follow symbolic links**, so a repository cannot make it read files outside itself.
- A diff line that merely *looks* like a file header cannot hide the lines after it (the parser counts the lines of each hunk instead of trusting their text).
- Reports, SARIF files and baselines **never contain a secret**.

## What it does not do

It does not call the provider to check whether a key is still valid, does not decode base64 or archives, and does not catch a secret split over several lines. [Every limitation, with numbers](docs/limitations.md).

## Commands

```
secscan scan [PATH] [--staged | --history] [--format text|json|sarif] [-o FILE]
             [--baseline FILE] [--exclude PATTERN]... [--max-size KIB] [--exit-zero]
secscan baseline [PATH] [-o FILE] [--history] [--exclude PATTERN]...
secscan install-hook [PATH] [--force]
secscan rules
```

## Development

```bash
python -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate      # Windows: .venv\Scripts\python.exe -m ...
pip install -e ".[dev]"
ruff check . && ruff format --check . && mypy && pytest --cov
python tools/evaluate.py --corpus /usr/lib/python3.13
```

The tests contain no literal credentials: [`tests/fakes.py`](tests/fakes.py) builds them at run time, so the repository never trips GitHub push protection, and `secscan` can scan itself (CI does).

